<BaseLayout
  title="Map and status policy — Geo Index"
  description="How Geo Index treats names, classifications, and boundaries on the globe and in country profiles."
  canonical="/methodology/maps/"
>
  <h1>Map and status policy</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    This atlas is a statistical and cartographic reference. It is not a recognition of legal
    claims, and it does not take a national point of view as its default world map.
  </p>

  <dl class="policy">
    <dt>Names and classifications</dt>
    <dd>
      Directory and profile headers always show a descriptive classification: UN member, UN
      observer, associated state, dependency, special administrative region, territory, statistical
      area, or special status. Classification is not a sovereignty verdict.
    </dd>
    <dt>Default geometry</dt>
    <dd>
      The globe uses Natural Earth’s de facto cartography, except where disputed areas are overlaid
      so that observer and special-status entities remain independently selectable — including
      Palestine, Taiwan, Kosovo, and Western Sahara. We do not ship a national point-of-view
      tileset as the default.
    </dd>
    <dt>Dispute lines</dt>
    <dd>
      Dispute lines stay visible and explained. They are not restyled into ordinary international
      borders, and fills do not imply a legal outcome.
    </dd>
    <dt>Hidden-area policy</dt>
    <dd>
      Populated, statistically tracked areas are not omitted because they are politically awkward.
    </dd>
    <dt>Natural Earth</dt>
    <dd>Made with Natural Earth. Geometry is public domain.</dd>
  </dl>
</BaseLayout>
